Let $l$ satisfy $l / e$ equal to the distance from focus to directrix, so $l = a abs(1 - e^2)$. Polar coordinates $(r, theta)$ centred on a focus turn the focus-directrix condition into

$ r = e ((l)/(e) - r cos theta) <=> r = (l)/(1 + e cos theta). $

Hence an ellipse ($e < 1$) has $r = (a (1 - e^2))/(1 + e cos theta)$, a hyperbola ($e > 1$) has $r = (a (e^2 - 1))/(1 + e cos theta)$, and a parabola ($e = 1$) has $r = (2 a)/(1 + cos theta)$.
